<u>02-SEP-2005 10:20 PM (AUS Time)</u><br>
Unlike yesterday, today turned out to be a fabulous day. The weather didn't start out perfect, but it sure ended up that way.  Our plans today, were to head all the way up to Cape Tribulation, a really good beach I'm told.  Along the way, we would stop off at Mossman Gorge, and the Daintree River, and then Cape Tribulation.
<p>
The trip today didn't seem that long at all. Of course he had left at 7 AM today unlike the day before so we had a lot more time if we needed it.  We got up to Mossman Gorge and once again I was expecting something pretty cool, but instead it just reminded me of the Eagle Creek Trail out in Columbia Gorge. In fact Eagle Creek was better.  We had been following up the river and also walking through the rainforest. Looking up you could see what was called a fern basket which was literally a basket of ferns growing high above the ground in the trees.  I had overheard another tour guide saying that the ferns basically grow upward towards the light and when they can't get any, they will move themselves upwards up a tree and this is how the fern baskets were formed.  Pretty interesting stuff.  We finally got to a spot on the river which looked pretty cool.  It was an area where the large rocks were smoothed and the water had that bright green algae in it.  At the end was a rope swing bridge which we crossed and then headed back.  There wasn't really much to Mossman Gorge but it was a nice little break in our trip up to Cape Tribulation.
<p>
The next stop was at the Daintree Rainforest.  The Daintree river is known to have crocs in it as well as other creatures like tree snakes and pythons.  Certainly not a place you would want to go hiking in.  When we got there, there were several tour companies but I let M pick and she picked a winner.  We went with a custom made craft that ran on solar power and had and ultra quiet motor so you can get close to wildlife without scaring it away.  Plus it also contained a high zooming camcorder connected to a TV and the guide was really good at spotting wildlife and would zoom up close on them so you could see it without straining your neck.  We managed to see plenty of tree snakes and birds, a python or two, but only one baby croc.  We went in further and saw another croc sticking just its head out of the water.  I was beginning to think that the one baby croc was going to be it as the guide was telling us that when it rains hard, the crocs all go in the water.  But we lucked out on the way back.  A huge croc was sitting on the bank. It was old and had no teeth left, so they called it Gummy.  But don't make the mistake of thinking you are safe,  Gummy can still exert 3000 lbs of pressure with his gummy jaws.   Seeing Gummy really made the tour exceptional.
<p>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1126437628_2.jpg>-->
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1126437628_2.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1126437628_2.jpg"></a>
<p> 
The next stop would be Cape Tribulation, but the only way to get there was to cross the Daintree river. And the only way to do that was by a car ferry. This particular ferry was connected to two cables and ferry would only follow this particular path by pulling on the cables.  
<p>
After we crossed, we was only about another 40 min before we would finally arrive at Cape Tribulation. In particular, the beach we arrive at was called Myall Beach.  Before we got started, we decided to do a 30 min walking tour through the rainforest just to get a feel for what the plants and wildlife look like. The path through the rainforest led out to the beach and just as we could see the beach, we were quite amazed at what we saw.
<p>
The tide was low and the coral was exposed.  All along the beach. Coolest thing I've seen on the trip so far.  M and I decided to take a closer look.  You could see bits of brain coral but all along the beach you could see these little snakelike creatures washed up on the sure.  I had never seen that before.  And all these spiral shells which looked like the dead hardened skin of a snake.  I don't know what it was.  In some of the puddles of water, you could see tiny fish and plants.  Then of course, it started to pour rain again so we headed back to the car.
<p>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1126437628_3.jpg>-->
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1126437628_3.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1126437628_3.jpg"></a>
<p>
The drive back was along a pretty stretch of windy highway along the beach and made for quite a scenic drive.  We decided to stop off at the town of Port Douglas because we had heard it was one of the best stops for diving the reef.  However, as we got there, we were a little turned off by the extremely touristy aspect of big name resorts and condos and hordes of rich people.  We decided we would just stick with Cairns for diving.
